  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on the `crates/trust-but-verify/src/resource.rs` root is an acceptable file-level coverage exception.\n\n- **Source matches the stated reason.** The file declares only `ResourceId` and `OutlineId`, with derives and no handwritten functions. `crates/trust-but-verify/src/lib.rs` declares it as the private `mod resource;`, which agrees with the \"private compatibility module\" claim. The commentary is in fenced citation commentary, so it counts as a tracked justification.\n- **Callers match the claim about legacy tests.** The `dispatch` function in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/sources.rs` builds `ResourceId` from JSON arguments and then builds `OutlineId`. Tests in `agent/tests.rs` observe `OutlineId` values, for example `source_read_pages_retain_text_and_dependencies_without_another_read` and `source_tools_forward_arguments_and_present_discovery_and_read_results`. They also construct and serialize `ResourceId`, so the \"exercised through the legacy adapter\" claim holds. I read these only as search snippets, not full function bodies.\n- **The separate path-based test exists.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs` has `addresses_reconstruct_without_reading_the_file`, which checks path-based address construction. It asserts `id.path` and `id.selector`, so the claim that this is tested separately holds.\n- **Derived-code claim is plausible.** The statement that derived serialization and identity operations emit no source-line measurements is credible for derive-generated code. I did not verify it against coverage tooling.\n\nThe citation is also not redundant: the file has no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to `habeas:coverage`.",
